<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Wagram</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fifth-coalition">War of the Fifth Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Wagram was fought on 5–6 July 1809 on the Marchfeld plain north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and the Austrian army of Archduke Charles. After crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the French won in two days of fighting, one of the largest battles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> up to that time, with about 300,000 men engaged. The defeat led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to sign the armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znaim</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a>, ceding large territories.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram.jpg" alt="Battle of Wagram" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napoleon_Wagram.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had moved a number of soldiers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> saw its chance to recover some of its former sphere of influence and invaded the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a French ally. Napoleon beat the Austrian forces and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> at the beginning of May 1809, but Archduke Charles of Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> salvaged an army and retreated north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, so the war went on.</p><p>Towards the end of May, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> suffered a surprise defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-aspern-essling">Battle of Aspern-Essling</a>, which cost some 53,000 casualties, almost equally divided between the two armies. It took him six weeks to prepare his next offensive, for which he gathered a French, German and Italian army of over 150,000 men near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle began after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> with the bulk of his forces during the night of 4 July and attacked the Austrian army, which had over 130,000 men. On the first day he tried for an early breakthrough with a series of evening attacks. The Austrians were thinly spread in a wide semicircle but held a naturally strong position, and the attacks failed. Napoleon knew by that night that he had sustained high losses.</p><p>At dawn on 6 July Archduke Charles launched attacks along the whole line, hoping to take the French in a double envelopment. The offensive failed against the French right but nearly broke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s left. He redeployed IV Corps under Masséna to steady that wing and set up a grand battery, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Marshal Davout</a> attacked the Austrian left. With his flank collapsing and no reserves left, Charles ordered an orderly retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was the uncontested winner but did not secure an overwhelming victory. With more than 300,000 combatants, it was the largest battle in European history up to that time, and with at least 72,000 casualties it was the bloodiest engagement of the French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> thus far. The cause was a concentration of artillery greater than any before on a flat battlefield; each army fired at least 90,000 roundshot. French casualties numbered 34,000, including over thirty generals.</p><p>On 7 July Napoleon made General MacDonald a Marshal of the Empire on the field, the only man so raised, and later gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</a> the title of 1st Prince of Wagram. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-schonbrunn">Treaty of Schönbrunn</a> meant the loss of one sixth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>'s subjects.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Wagram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Napoleonic Wars it suffered repeated defeats, at Ulm and Austerlitz in 1805 and <b>Wagram</b> in 1809, but under Archduke Charles it inflicted on Napoleon his first major defeat in battle at Aspern-Essling in May 1809.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1807 Napoleon created the Duchy of Warsaw as a French client state from land taken from Prussia, and enlarged it at Austria's expense after the <b>Battle of Wagram</b> in 1809.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
